The selected software-video sources include Adrien Descamps' yuv2rgb code
under BSD-3-Clause. Its complete copyright, conditions and disclaimer are
retained in yuv2rgb-LICENSE alongside this notice. Static consumers must
retain that notice with SDL3's root licence and the Pyxis notices below.
The selected fdlibm sources retain Sun Microsystems' 1993 permission grant,
preserved verbatim from src/libm/s_sin.c in fdlibm-NOTICE. The licence label
LicenseRef-Sun-fdlibm refers to that complete notice.

Pyxis builds upstream sources as a static library with ordered patches:
pthread condition timeouts use the monotonic clock, file times come only from
valid Pyxis stat fields, the environment is read from the native table, the
Pyxis video driver is registered, and Pyxis pointer positions are
authoritative in SDL mouse core. The patches retain SDL's zlib license.

The Pyxis backend in pyxis/ (video, OpenGL, input, threads and paths) and
SDL_build_config.h are original Pyxis material under the Mozilla Public
License, version 2.0; see the ports repository's LICENSE and LICENSING.md.
The backend uses the shared US key layout exported by the Pyxis SDK.

The ordinary libSDL3.a has no OpenGL backend or Mesa/C++ dependency. The
separate libSDL3-osmesa.a compiles the Pyxis backend with PYXIS_USE_OSMESA and
binds statically to Mesa; select it instead of the ordinary archive. Both use
the same SDL API and public headers. Covered backend source and build
configuration are retained in sdl3-dev at share/sources/sdl3; the OSMesa
development package depends on sdl3-dev, mesa-dev and libcxx-dev. Static
consumers must retain Mesa and C++ runtime notices as well.

Audio, clipboard, joysticks, haptics, sensors, HIDAPI, camera, GPU, shared
objects and Vulkan are not built. No host libc or compatibility stubs are
included.
